Yes. If you want to **permanently stop KVM from loading** so VirtualBox always has access to AMD-V, you can disable it.

### 1. Blacklist the KVM modules

```bash
echo "blacklist kvm" | sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-kvm.conf
echo "blacklist kvm_amd" | sudo tee -a /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-kvm.conf
```

### 2. Rebuild the initramfs (Manjaro)

```bash
sudo mkinitcpio -P
```

### 3. Reboot

```bash
sudo reboot
```

### 4. Verify after reboot

```bash
lsmod | grep kvm
```

If nothing is printed, KVM is no longer loaded and VirtualBox should be able to use AMD-V.

### If you ever want to re-enable KVM

```bash
sudo rm /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-kvm.conf
sudo mkinitcpio -P
sudo reboot
```

**Note:** This disables KVM/QEMU virtualization on your system. 
VirtualBox will work, but you won't be able to use KVM-based virtual machines until you remove the blacklist and reboot.
